# The narration, scene by scene (the client's script). id = scene number.
VO = [
"You walked in for one thing. Milk. The simplest item in the building, the one almost everyone buys. And somehow, it's sitting as far from the front door as the store can possibly put it.",
"To reach it, you pass the bread, the snacks, the flowers, the offers stacked at the end of every aisle. So the question writes itself. Who decided milk belongs at the back — and why?",
"There's a famous answer. You've probably heard it. And it's right — but only partly. Because the real story starts a century ago, with a milk cart, a block of ice, and a store shaped like a maze.",
"The famous answer goes like this. Milk is a staple. People buy it constantly, often several times a week. So if you put it at the back, every quick trip turns into a long walk.",
"Retailers have a phrase for what happens on that walk: building the basket. You came for one carton. You leave with biscuits, a candle and a rotisserie chicken you never planned to buy.",
"Store designers study this obsessively. Retail researcher Paco Underhill found shoppers barely register anything just inside the door. He called it the decompression zone — the stretch where your brain is still outside.",
"By this logic, the milk is bait. The longest walk in the shop is a sales funnel with a fridge at the end. It's tidy, it's cynical — and it's missing something important.",
"For most of history, there was no walk at all. In an early twentieth-century grocery, you stood at a counter, read out your list, and a clerk fetched everything from the shelves behind him.",
"And milk often didn't come from a shop at all. It came to your door. Milkmen with horse-drawn carts, and later electric floats, made daily rounds through cities from London to Chicago.",
"By the late sixties, Britain's milk floats made up the world's largest fleet of electric vehicles. In America, nearly thirty percent of consumers had milk delivered in nineteen sixty-three. By two thousand five: under half a percent.",
"Delivery worked because milk spoiled fast and most homes had nowhere truly cold to keep it. Two things would end that world: a new kind of store, and a cold box in every kitchen.",
"September sixth, nineteen sixteen. Memphis, Tennessee. A grocer named Clarence Saunders opens the first Piggly Wiggly and does something radical. He removes the clerk, and hands the shopper a basket.",
"Saunders patented his self-serving store in nineteen seventeen. Shoppers followed one continuous path, shelves on both sides, and they passed every product in the store before they reached the cashier.",
"That's the real ancestor of the milk-at-the-back theory. The first self-service store was designed, on purpose, to walk you past everything. The walk was the business model from day one.",
"But in that maze, milk had no special spot. Most stores had no mechanical refrigeration yet. Dairy sat in iceboxes, or came off a wagon. The back wall wasn't cold. That was about to change.",
"Through the nineteen-thirties, the electric refrigerator went from luxury to normal. In the United States, ownership jumped from about eight percent of homes to around forty-four percent in a single decade.",
"A fridge at home meant you could buy a week of milk at once. The daily doorstep round began to fade, and the weekly shopping trip was born — by car, to a much bigger store.",
"August nineteen thirty. In an empty garage in Queens, New York, Michael Cullen opens King Kullen: huge, cheap, mostly self-service, with parking. Many historians call it America's first true supermarket.",
"Big stores needed refrigerated display cases, and those machines came with compressors, condensers and pipework — heavy, noisy and hot behind. Nobody wanted that by the front door. So the cold went to the back.",
"Now follow the milk itself. It leaves the dairy in a refrigerated truck, held at around four degrees Celsius or colder, and arrives at the one door every supermarket has: the loading dock, at the back.",
"Milk is heavy. A single US gallon weighs nearly four kilos, and stores move it by the pallet, often with several deliveries a week. Every metre it travels costs labour, and costs cold.",
"So stores shrink that trip to almost nothing. Behind the dairy wall sits a walk-in cold room. Staff roll pallets straight in from the dock, and fill the shelves from behind.",
"That's why the dairy section feels like a wall. It literally is one. The milk fridge is the front of a cold room with glass doors cut into it — and that cold room has to touch the dock.",
"Look at convenience stores, from New York to Tokyo. There are no long aisles to march you past, yet the milk still lines the back or side wall. The cooler goes where the cold room is.",
"So logistics put the milk at the back. But once it was there, store designers realised they'd been handed a gift. And they started designing the whole store around it.",
"Many supermarkets follow a loop around the edge, sometimes called the racetrack. Fresh food lines the perimeter. Packaged goods fill the aisles in the middle. And the everyday staples sit at the far end.",
"Fresh produce often greets you at the entrance. It's colourful, it smells good, and it makes the whole shop feel fresher. And the smell of the bakery drifts exactly where you'll catch it.",
"Then come the end caps, the displays at the end of each aisle. Products placed there can sell far more than the same product mid-aisle. And the walk to the milk passes nearly all of them.",
"Eye level is buy level. Brands pay for the best shelf spots, and children's cereal often sits lower, at a child's eye line. Every step toward the milk is another chance to be seen.",
"Notice too that milk, eggs and bread are rarely neighbours. Spread them out, and one short list sends you round the whole track. The truck chose the back wall. The marketer chose the route.",
"If this were only a trick, every store would play it. Not all do. Paco Underhill noticed supermarkets adding small grab-and-go sections near the entrance, for shoppers who just want in and out.",
"Walk into a small city-centre store in London, Paris or Sydney, and you'll sometimes find milk in a chiller just inside the door. These stores serve commuters who want it in thirty seconds.",
"But in the big weekly-shop stores, from Chicago to Manchester to Melbourne, dairy still sits along the back or side wall. Big stores, big deliveries, big cold rooms. Same answer every time.",
"Where milk can move to the front, it sometimes does. Where it can't, it stays at the back. That pattern tells you which force came first: the geography of cold, not just the psychology of shoppers.",
"Economist Russ Roberts made a sharper point. Moving the milk to the front isn't impossible. It means freestanding coolers, longer refrigeration lines and more restocking work. And someone has to pay for that.",
"And shoppers, given the choice, tend to pick lower prices over a shorter walk. Stores that kept costs down stayed busy. In a way, we voted for the long walk ourselves.",
"So: truck or marketer? Both, in that order. The truck put the milk at the back, because cold is expensive. The marketer kept it there, because your walk is valuable.",
"Next time you walk in for one carton, you'll know what you're really doing: retracing a delivery route, a cooling pipe, and a hundred years of retail design, one aisle at a time.",
"Because from up here, the path you follow every week was never really random. It was shaped, from the very start, by the thing you came in for.",
"So here's a trick of your own. Make a list, grab a basket instead of a trolley, and walk straight to the back. The store built a racetrack. You don't have to run the whole lap.",
]
